@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')Differences from Laravel@endsection
@section('description')Where Orbit departs from Laravel's Eloquent and query builder: first without a default order, fillable checks, insert's returned id, NULL comparisons, replace, transactions, errors and table names.@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>Orbit follows Laravel's Eloquent and query builder, with these exceptions. Each one is listed here so it is not a surprise to someone who knows Laravel.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>first()</code> has no default order.</strong> As in Laravel, <code>first()</code> takes the first row the database returns. Call <code>order_by</code> when the row matters. Laravel's <code>sole</code>, <code>chunk</code> and <code>lazy</code> enforce an order; Orbit does not add one.</li>
    <li><strong>Columns outside <code>FILLABLE</code> are an error.</strong> <code>insert</code> and <code>update</code> return <code>Error::NotFillable</code> for a column that is not in the model's <code>FILLABLE</code> list. Laravel's <code>Model::create</code> drops such an attribute without a message, so a misspelt column is ignored. Orbit stops instead, so the mistake shows up at once. Laravel's query builder does not check <code>$fillable</code> at all; Orbit checks it on every write.</li>
    <li><strong><code>insert</code> returns the id.</strong> It returns the auto-increment id as <code>u64</code>. Laravel's query builder <code>insert</code> returns a boolean, and <code>insertGetId</code> returns the id.</li>
    <li><strong><code>where_eq(column, None)</code> is <code>IS NULL</code></strong>, and <code>Operator::Ne</code> with <code>None</code> is <code>IS NOT NULL</code>, as in Laravel. Other operators with <code>None</code> match nothing, where Laravel throws.</li>
    <li><strong>Terminal methods take the database.</strong> <code>get(&amp;db)</code> and the other terminal methods take the <code>Database</code> explicitly. Laravel uses a default connection.</li>
    <li><strong>Transactions take an async closure that receives the transaction.</strong> <code>Database::transaction</code> passes the transaction as <code>tx</code>, and queries run on it when given <code>&amp;mut *tx</code>. Laravel's <code>DB::transaction</code> takes no argument and uses the default connection. The closure's error type must convert from <code>Error</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Transactions do not retry.</strong> Laravel's <code>DB::transaction($callback, $attempts)</code> retries on a deadlock. Orbit returns <code>Error::Deadlock</code> and leaves the retry to the caller.</li>
    <li><strong>Grouped conditions are <code>where_group</code> and <code>or_where_group</code>.</strong> They take a closure that receives an empty query, as Laravel's <code>where(fn)</code> does. Only the conditions are kept from the group; <code>order_by</code>, <code>limit</code> and the other settings inside it are ignored.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors are typed.</strong> Unique and foreign key violations, deadlocks and lock wait timeouts are variants of <code>Error</code>. Laravel's <code>QueryException</code> carries the SQLSTATE code instead. A unique violation's index name comes from the server's message, because the MySQL driver does not return it as a separate field.</li>
    <li><strong><code>replace</code> is Diesel's <code>replace_into</code>, not Laravel's <code>upsert</code>.</strong> <code>Query::replace</code> issues MySQL's <code>REPLACE INTO</code>, with Diesel's semantics. It deletes the row that has the same unique key or primary key, then inserts the new one. So the auto-increment id changes, columns not in the values take their defaults, and the returned count is the rows affected as MySQL reports them (2 for a replaced row). Laravel's <code>upsert</code> is <code>INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE</code>, which updates the existing row in place and keeps its id. That is a different operation, and Orbit does not implement it.</li>
    <li><strong>The blocking API refuses async threads.</strong> <code>orbit_orm::blocking</code> (feature <code>blocking</code>) runs the async queries on a runtime that the <code>Database</code> owns. Laravel has no sync or async split, so this follows Diesel, which Ignite uses. A blocking call on a thread that runs async code returns <code>Error::BlockingInRuntime</code>, rather than panicking inside Tokio or stalling the runtime. Call it from <code>tokio::task::spawn_blocking</code>, as Ignite does. Each call blocks its thread, so the blocking pool is the place for it. The <code>Database</code> shuts its runtime down in the background when dropped, so dropping it on an async thread is safe.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Matches Laravel, with limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>for_update</code> runs outside a transaction.</strong> Outside a transaction, <code>for_update</code> runs <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> in autocommit, and the lock lasts for that statement. This matches Laravel's <code>lockForUpdate()</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>The default table name follows Laravel's inflector, with gaps.</strong> Without <code>table</code>, the name is <code>Str::snake(Str::pluralStudly(name))</code>, as in Eloquent: only the last word is pluralized, so <code>UserProfile</code> gives <code>user_profiles</code> and <code>Person</code> gives <code>people</code>. The plural rules are ported from doctrine/inflector 2.1.x, the English rules Laravel's <code>Pluralizer</code> uses, with the same uncountable words, irregular words and suffix rules in the same order. Checked against Laravel v13.42.0 (<code>illuminate/support</code> 13.42.0), which requires <code>doctrine/inflector ^2.0.5</code> and resolves to 2.1.1: 1,693 distinct class names (1,862 rows over two lists) give the same <code>Str::snake(Str::pluralStudly(name))</code> as Orbit. Before the fixes from that check, 13 of them differed (<code>Media</code>, <code>People</code>, <code>Trivia</code>, <code>Maltese</code> and <code>Testis</code>, alone and in compounds); those are fixed. Gaps: the rules are ASCII only, so a name that ends in a non-ASCII letter gets a plain <code>s</code>; the check covered doctrine/inflector 2.1.1 only, and names outside the tested set are not proven to match every doctrine rule; a word that matches no rule gets an <code>s</code>, as Laravel does. <code>table = "..."</code> overrides the default, and an explicit name is used as written.</li>
  </ul>
@endsection
